/** IS THE PAGE ON ITS WAY OUT?
 *
 * A deliberate navigation kills every request in flight, and the browser reports that as `TypeError: Failed to fetch`
 * — indistinguishable, at the catch site, from a server that refused. So the most successful action an app has
 * (`Session.SignIn`, which stores the ticket and then `location.assign`s to the landing page) ended by logging
 * **"Action ui:SignupPage.Submit failed"** at Error. Measured 2026-08-20 in a real browser against an app that had
 * just passed the whole eval suite: the user was signed in, on the right page, and the console said the action broke.
 *
 * That is worse than noise. The action-failure line is the highest-value line an app emits, and a false one in EVERY
 * app that has a login teaches the reader to ignore the channel — after which a real failure looks the same.
 *
 * ⚠ THE WINDOW IS DELIBERATELY NARROW, because the failure mode of getting this wrong is swallowing a genuine error.
 * Nothing here suppresses anything: the throw still propagates untouched and the log is still WRITTEN. Only its LEVEL
 * changes, and only once the app has actually asked the browser to leave.
 */
let navigating = false;

/** The app has asked the browser to leave this page. Called by the sign-in / sign-out redirects and by `pagehide`. */
export function markNavigating(): void { navigating = true; }

/** True once a navigation has been initiated — after which an aborted request is expected, not a fault. */
export function isNavigating(): boolean { return navigating; }

/** Tests only: a module-level latch would otherwise leak between cases. */
export function resetNavigating(): void { navigating = false; }
