            // walked into `root` was ever sent, let alone persisted. Per the documented contract (`Docs/reference/
            // function/unit-of-work.md` §Failure-leaves-nothing-behind + `function-try-catch.md`'s own compiled
            // test, "the try block's row did NOT survive"): a refused commit discards EVERYTHING it was about to
            // carry, atomically — not just the fields that broke a rule. Leaving it in `root` stranded a ghost past
            // the reach of the enclosing `try`'s own rollback (its child savepoint is EMPTY by now — its content is
            // what we just promoted) — so a `live var` query of that type kept rendering a row the server never
            // saw, forever, because nothing downstream of this throw ever visits `root` again. Discard before
            // throwing: the fault's own `violations` array (captured above) still carries the per-field reasons to
            // the app's `catch`, so nothing about the diagnostic is lost — only the phantom row is.
            root.discard();
            throw new OsyFault(ValidationExceptionType, summarize(violations), violations);
